For the OOP-style underscore functions.
_.compose(*functions);
Technically can't support at the moment, as it returns a function that wraps all the functions where the output goes into the input of the next function
e.g.

var greet    = function(name){ return "hi: " + name; };
var exclaim  = function(statement){ return statement.toUpperCase() + "!"; };
var welcome = _.compose(greet, exclaim);
welcome('moe');
=> 'hi: MOE!'

For now will just treat it as a series of lambdas that we call, to at least go into the functions

CharacterizationRule.Definition:
foreach lambdas {
    Calls(stmt, lambdas, this)
}
Calls(stmt, lambda0, this)

CharacterizationRule.Notes:
Can inject expressions _.template(templateString, [settings]); For client-side use and server-side (templates are rendered server-side in Node.js);
For node.js we try to use possibleHeapPaths, for client-side we check '_' is the instance && !possibleHeapPaths
Node.js variant

For the OOP-style underscore functions.
_.times(n, iteratee, [context]);
Calls iteratee n times, each time with an index and gets an output of the array of the values returned from iteratee.

OOP-style _.every(), etc:
_(list).every([predicate], [context]);
works similar to _.find(), except it just returns a false value if false is ever returned from predicate, so don't need a CallsReturns rule
Can actually take an optional, but we don't care about that case, since it's effectively:

for (var foo : list){
    if (foo != true)
      return false;
}
return true;

OOP-style _.where(), etc:
_(list).where(properties) and _(list).findWhere(properties);
Returns the values in list that match the associated properties. Technically the information from arg0 is only returned if also in the array passed to _(), so will just have a passthrough this->return

For the OOP-style underscore functions.
_.wrap(function, wrapper)
Technically can't support at the moment, as it returns a function that sends the first function as a callback argument to the wrapper function
e.g.

var hello = function(name) { return "hello: " + name; };
hello = _.wrap(hello, function(func) {
  return "before, " + func("moe") + ", after";
});
hello();
=> 'before, hello: moe, after'

For now will just treat it as a lambda taking the 0th arg as input

