CharacterizationRule.Notes:
For case:

app.get('/', function(req, res){
    res.render('index.myExtension', {header: req.param('header') }, function(err, html){
        res.end(html);
    });
}

app.engine('myExtension', function(path, options, callback){
    options.header = options.header || "Hello World";
    callback(null, "<html><head></head><body><h1>" + options.header + "</h1></body></html>"); //possible XSS
}

Ideally would have a way to specify that res.render() calls the functions associated with app.engine, but I figure this would require labelling support in JS with HOA.
For the moment we'll generally flag when a templating engine is being used that's susceptible to simple XSS if the options are tainted. This could also be template injection really, hopefully we'd see when the potential user input is evaluated.

Specifying source as +XSS +WEB, I figure this is most likely as the response.render() call will always be made from a callback that also contains a request object, plus we can't specify a specific taint for this, as we also want to find potential problems elsewhere in the templating engine (such as templating injection specified above).

CharacterizationRule.StructuralMatch:
FunctionCall fc: possibleTargets contains [Function:
        name == "engine"
        and enclosingClass.supers contains [Class:
            name == "Application"
        ]
    ]
    and fc.arguments.length == 2
    and fc.arguments[1] is [Expression lambda:
        lambda.possibleFunctionTargets contains [Function:
            parameters[1] is [Variable p: ]
        ]
    ]

