CharacterizationRule.StructuralMatch:
AccessLocation al: al.accessName matches "params|pathParams"
    and (
        al.possibleHeapPaths contains "$route.current"
        or (al.accessInstance is [AccessLocation: name == "current"
            and accessInstance is [Location: name == "$route"]
        ])
    )
    and not al in [AssignmentStatement: lhs.location is
        [Location l: l.transitiveBase === al.transitiveBase]
    ]

AccessLocation al: al.accessName matches "params|pathParams"
    and (
        al.possibleHeapPaths contains "$route.current"
        or (al.accessInstance is [AccessLocation: name == "current"
            and accessInstance is [Location: name == "$route"]
        ])
    )
    and not al in [AssignmentStatement: lhs.location is
        [Location l: l.transitiveBase === al.transitiveBase]
    ]

FieldAccess fa: fa.field.name matches "params|pathParams"
    and (
        fa.possibleHeapPaths contains "$route.current"
        or (fa.instance is [FieldAccess fa2: field.name == "current"
            and fa2.instance is [FieldAccess: field.name == "$route"]
        ])
    )
    and not fa in [AssignmentStatement: lhs.location is
        [Location l: l.transitiveBase === fa.transitiveBase]
    ]

CharacterizationRule.Notes:
$sceDelegate.valueOf(value) takes a parameter. If this has gone through $sce.trustAs() (or one of the variants), it will return the original value that was passed to teh trustAs() call. Effectively this is vulnerable to XSS:
ng-bind-html="foo"
...
$scope.foo = $sce.trustAsHtml(payload);
This isn't:
$scope.foo = $sceDelegate.valueOf($sce.trustAsHtml(payload));

